{
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "The lecturer calculates a posterior probability of 2% that there is a thief in the house given the radio announcement of an earthquake.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "At [01:26:23\u201301:26:51], the lecturer states: *\"it appeared that there was just 2% of probability that there was really a thief in my house.\"* This is a direct statement of the calculated posterior probability after incorporating radio evidence."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The lecturer assumes independence between earthquakes and thieves in the probabilistic model.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "At [01:27:07\u201301:27:44], the lecturer states: *\"in my model, there was such causal graph, which assumed that earthquakes and thieves happen independently on one another.\"\nThis explicitly states the model assumption of independence between earthquakes and thieves."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Thieves respond to radio announcements of earthquakes by increasing their activity, which creates a dependency between earthquakes and thieves.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "At [01:27:44\u201301:28:13], the lecturer states: *\"thieves are not idiots. They also listen to the radio. And if they listen that there was a small earthquake, they immediately increase their thief activity because they know that now different orbs that will be generated will be interpreted as a result of a small earthquake rather than as a result of thief penetration.\"\nThis explains the real-world dependency that the model did not account for."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The posterior probability of a thief in the house is lower when there is an earthquake announcement than when there is no earthquake announcement.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "At [01:27:44\u201301:28:13], the lecturer states: *\"the probability that there is a thief, if there was, if there was earthquake is larger than probability of thief if there was no earthquake.\"\nThis indicates that the conditional probability of a thief given an earthquake is higher than the conditional probability of a thief given no earthquake, which is the opposite of what the model assumed."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The model misspecification led to the apartment being robbed, as the lecturer states that the model was too optimistic.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "At [01:27:44\u201301:28:13], the lecturer states: *\"This is what every sociologist... It will tell you that this is true, that if some accident happens, then the crime activity goes up. So I did not take this back into account. That's why my model became too optimistic, and probably that's why my appointment was robbed.\"\nThis directly links the model misspecification to the robbery."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The lecturer uses a Bayesian framework to model the relationship between earthquakes, thieves, and radio announcements.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "At [01:23:46\u201301:24:29], the lecturer states: *\"that the thief is in my house based on the alarm system that has sent me SMS and based on radio message that announced about earthquake. So here again, I will simply do by definition. So this is conditional probability by definition. This is joint probability or probability of condition.\"\nThis shows the lecturer using Bayesian methods to compute probabilities."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The lecturer calculates the joint probability over variables T (thief), E (earthquake), R (radio), and A (alarm) using the sum rule.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "At [01:24:33\u201301:25:19], the lecturer states: *\"Remember that I do have access to the joint probability of all variables. So now to compute joint probability over T, E, R, A, R. And now I need to compute numerator and denominator. How to express both numerator and denominator via this joint distribution. Well, based on the sum rule of code. So I take T equal to one, E.\n[01:25:19\u201301:25:56] r equal to 1, a equal to 1, r equal to 1, and they integrate it out with respect to all possible values of e.\"\nThis describes the calculation method using the sum rule."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The lecturer states that the Bayesian framework extends classical Boolean logic.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "At [01:28:44\u201301:29:28], the lecturer states: *\"And this is an example of how we can perform so-called Bayesian reasoning, which in some sense extends the classical Boolean logic.\"\nThis is a direct statement about the extension of Boolean logic by Bayesian reasoning."
    }
  ],
  "clarity_1_to_5": 4,
  "caveat": "The lecturer explicitly states that the model assumed independence between earthquakes and thieves, but in reality, there is a dependency (thieves respond to radio announcements of earthquakes). This dependency causes the posterior probability to be too low (2%) when the model doesn't account for it. The lecturer also notes that the model was too optimistic, which led to the apartment being robbed."
  }
